<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sambandar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Thirugnana Sambandar, Tirujnana Sambandar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sambandar, Thirugnana Sambandar, was a Tamil Shaiva poet-saint of the 7th century, one of the most revered of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a>. Tradition says he began composing hymns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as a small child and died young; his hymns form the first three books of the Tevaram. According to Shaiva tradition he converted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, an episode that symbolises the decline of Jainism in the Tamil country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sambandar.jpg" alt="Sambandar" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (late 14th century artist) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hindu_Saint_Sambandar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
